Qi Liu] ,\nL67: \nL68: [Button: Haitao Mi] ,\nL69: \nL70: [Button: Dong Yu]\nL71: ## Abstract\nL72: \nL73: Two methods, dynamic CoT switching and Diverse parallel-scaled RL, reduce computational cost in ATP models while maintaining performance.\nL74: \nL75: Generated by cite29†Qwen/Qwen2.5-Coder-32B-Instruct L76: Large Language Models (LLMs) have recently advanced the field of Automated Theorem Proving (ATP), attaining substantial performance gains through widely adopted test-time scaling strategies, notably reflective cite30†Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning and increased cite31†sampling passes . However, they both introduce significant computational overhead for inference.\nL77: Moreover, existing cost analyses typically regulate only the number of cite31†sampling passes , while neglecting the substantial disparities in sampling costs introduced by different scaling strategies. In this paper, we systematically compare the efficiency of different test-time scaling strategies for ATP models and demonstrate the inefficiency of the current state-of-the-art (SOTA) open-source approaches.\nL78: We then investigate approaches to significantly reduce cite32†token usage and sample passes while maintaining the original performance.\nL79: Specifically, we propose two complementary methods that can be integrated into a unified cite33†EconRL pipeline for amplified benefits: (1) a dynamic cite30†Chain-of-Thought (CoT) switching mechanism designed to mitigate unnecessary token consumption, and (2) Diverse parallel-scaled cite34†reinforcement learning (RL) with trainable prefixes to enhance pass rates under constrained cite31†sampling passes .\nL80: Experiments on cite35†miniF2F and cite36†ProofNet demonstrate that our cite37†EconProver achieves comparable performance to baseline methods with only 12% of the computational cost. This work provides actionable insights for deploying lightweight ATP models without sacrificing performance.\nL81: cite38†View arXiv page†arxiv.org cite39†View PDF†arxiv.org cite27†Add to collection L82: ### Community\nL83: \nL84: cite28†Image L85: \nL86: cite26†kiaia L87: \nL88: Paper author Paper submitter cite40†Sep 17, 2025 L89: \nL90: 🎓 Checkout our new: EconProver: Towards More Economical Test-Time Scaling for Automated Theorem Proving ✍️\nL91: \nL92: 🚀 Large Language Models (LLMs) are pushing boundaries in ATP via techniques like reflective CoT reasoning & high sample counts — but at steep inference costs.\nL93: \nL94: ⚙️ EconProver proposes a more ⚡ efficient pipeline:\nL95:   * Dynamic CoT switching → avoid unnecessary tokens.\nL96:   * Diverse parallel-scaled RL with trainable prefixes → get more “proofs per pass” under tight sampling constraints.\nL97: \nL98: 📊 On miniF2F & ProofNet: comparable baseline performance with only 12% of the computational cost.\nL99: \nL100: 🌟 Why it matters: allows lightweight ATP models to remain performant while cutting inference costs. Important for scaling theorem proving in resource-constrained settings.\nL101: \nL102: 🚀\nL103: \nL104: 2\nL105: \nL106: 2\nL107: \nL108: 🔥\nL109: \nL110: 1\nL111: \nL112: 1\nL113: \nL114: +\nL115: \nL116: Reply\nL117: \nL118: cite41†Image†cdn-avatars.huggingface.co L119: cite42†librarian-bot L120: \nL121: cite43†Sep 18, 2025 L122: \nL123: This is an automated message from the cite44†Librarian Bot . I found the following papers similar to this paper.\nL124: \nL125: The following papers were recommended by the Semantic Scholar API\nL126:   * cite45†Goedel-Prover-V2: Scaling Formal Theorem Proving with Scaffolded Data Synthesis and Self-Correction (2025)\nL127:   * cite46†Pruning the Unsurprising: Efficient Code Reasoning via First-Token Surprisal (2025)\nL128:   * cite47†Scaling Up, Speeding Up: A Benchmark of Speculative Decoding for Efficient LLM Test-Time Scaling (2025)\nL129:   * cite48†Step-level Verifier-guided Hybrid Test-Time Scaling for Large Language Models (